+## deployHookUrl is untrusted input
+
+`deployHookUrl` is a plain-text field an author fills in, which makes it as
+untrusted as any other user-supplied fetch target — nothing stops it from
+pointing at `localhost`, an RFC 1918 address, or the cloud-metadata endpoint
+at `169.254.169.254` instead of a real static host. There's no host
+allowlist to fall back on, because the whole point of the field is to accept
+whatever provider a given customer happens to use.
+
+Instead, `triggerDeployHook` resolves the URL's host and rejects the call if
+the _resolved_ address lands in a reserved range (loopback, private, link-
+local/cloud-metadata, multicast) — see `assertSafeDeployHookUrl` in
+`deploy-hook.ts` for the exact ranges. That check runs again on every retry
+rather than once up front, so a DNS record that only rebinds to an internal
+address after the first attempt still gets caught. The request itself also
+disables redirects (`redirect: "manual"`), so a hook that 302s to an internal
+address doesn't get followed there. A URL that fails this check doesn't burn
+the remaining retries — retrying can't turn an unsafe destination safe, so it
+returns `{ ok: false }` immediately.
+
